<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Saud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud, Abdulaziz ibn Abdul Rahman Al Saud, Abdulaziz ibn Saud · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">House of Saud</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Saud, Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> ibn Abd al-Rahman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a>, recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> from the Rashidis in 1902 and rebuilt Saudi power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> in 1913, destroyed the Rashidi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Jabal Shammar in 1921 and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> in 1924–1925. In 1932 he united his lands as the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, where oil was found in 1938.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-saud.jpg" alt="Ibn Saud" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saudi Press Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Portrait_of_King_Abdulaziz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ibn Saud was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, the fourth child and third son of Abdul Rahman bin Faisal, one of the last rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>, the second Saudi state, a tribal sheikhdom centred on Riyadh. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> family had been a power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> for about 130 years. The family's long-term rivals, led by Muhammad bin Abdullah Al Rashid, conquered Riyadh when Ibn Saud was a boy, and the family took refuge with the Al Murrah, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribe, and then settled in Kuwait for nearly a decade, where he developed a rapport with the ruler Mubarak Al Sabah. His paternal aunt, Jawhara bint Faisal, told him of his ancestors' deeds and helped persuade him to return to Nejd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in 1902, and many former supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">House of Saud</a> rallied to him. Over the next two years his forces recaptured almost half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a> from the Rashidis. On 15 June 1904 combined Ottoman and Rashidi forces defeated him heavily, but he waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and in February 1905 they named him qaimmaqam of southern Nejd. His victory at Rawdat Muhanna ended the Ottoman presence in Nejd and Qassim by the end of October 1906.</p><p>Over three decades his conquests made him ruler of nearly all of central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. He consolidated control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> in 1921 and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> in 1925, and in 1932 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> became the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. He signed a friendship and cooperation pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1915. In the Saudi-Yemeni War of 1934 the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a> confirmed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizan">Jazan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a>. Oil was discovered in 1938, after he granted a concession in 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He kept Saudi Arabia neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. On 14 February 1945 he met the American president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> aboard USS Quincy on the Great Bitter Lake, a meeting that laid the basis of later relations between the two countries. He fathered almost one hundred children, including 45 sons. He died in his sleep at Shubra Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a> on 9 November 1953, with Prince Faisal at his side, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> next to his sister Noura. His sons have succeeded him as kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibn Saud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1902 Abdul Rahman's son Abdulaziz, later <b>Ibn Saud</b>, recaptured Riyadh and founded the third Saudi state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud</b> recaptured Riyadh in 1902 and the Emirate of Nejd and Hasa steadily pushed back the Rashidis, killing Abd al-Aziz ibn Mutib in 1906.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-jabal-shammar">Emirate of Jabal Shammar</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1902 the young <b>Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud</b>, living in exile in the Sheikhdom of Kuwait, captured Riyadh's Masmak fort from the Rashidi governor with a small band of followers.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nejd-and-hasa">Emirate of Nejd and Hasa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1926 Hasan al-Idrisi accepted a Saudi protectorate; <b>Ibn Saud</b> annexed the territory in 1930, and after a failed revolt and the Saudi-Yemeni war of 1934 it was absorbed into Saudi Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ibn Saud's</b> Ikhwan took Ta'if and then Mecca in late 1924.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Kingdom of Hejaz</a></li><li><blockquote>After conquering the Kingdom of Hejaz, <b>Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud</b> was proclaimed King of the Hejaz in Mecca in January 1926 while remaining Sultan of Nejd.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz-and-nejd">Kingdom of Hejaz and Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.64,46.72&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Riyadh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5791 1257 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ibn Saud"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1527h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1527h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.64,46.72&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
